.TH WISP-INIT 7 "2026-09-18" "wisp 0.3.0" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
wisp-init \- the /init of a wisp initramfs
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wisp-init
is one static program that brings a machine from "kernel running, nothing
mounted, root encrypted" to "the real operating system runs on the decrypted
root". It has no shell, no udev, no cryptsetup and no lvm2 inside. Seven steps:
.IP 1. 4
Mount proc, sysfs, devtmpfs and a tmpfs on /run.
.IP 1b. 4
Loads the console keyboard layout from
.IR /etc/wisp/keymap.bin ,
when the builder placed one (see
.BR wisp.conf (5)),
so the passphrase is typed in the system's layout.
.IP 2. 4
Coldplug: read every
.I modalias
under /sys/devices, match it against the alias patterns of the modules in the
archive (the kernel's own fnmatch semantics), load the matches with their
dependencies, and repeat until a round loads nothing new, because a bus driver
makes its children appear. If the LUKS container has not appeared, every
storage module in the archive is loaded; if no input device exists, every HID
and input module. Patterns and dependencies come from the module table
.I modules.wisp
the builder placed next to the modules; the init opens no module file, the
kernel decompresses it in finit_module.
.IP 3. 4
Wait for the LUKS2 container with the configured UUID, up to 30 seconds.
.IP 4. 4
Obtain the key according to
.B key
in
.BR wisp.conf (5):
none, passphrase, credential, or a key provider process.
.IP 5. 4
Hash the header's JSON area and compare it with the measured table
.I /etc/wisp/luks.tab
the builder wrote from it; a changed header halts with a message. Then
recover the volume key from a keyslot in pure Go, as the table describes,
and create the dm-crypt mapping through the device-mapper ioctl. The init
does not parse the header. If a volume table was recorded, replay it over
the dm-crypt device to activate the root logical volume.
.IP 6. 4
Find the root file system by UUID or path and mount it read-only.
.IP 7. 4
Move the pseudo file systems, chroot, and execute /sbin/init.
.PP
On any failure the init prints the reason and halts. There is no rescue shell:
an initramfs with a shell is an initramfs with a root shell for whoever holds
the keyboard. Power off and boot the previous kernel, whose initrd is left in
place when wisp is installed, or a rescue medium.
.SH WHAT IS AND IS NOT INSIDE
The archive holds the init, the resolved modules of exactly one kernel with
their module table and the firmware files they name (unless
.BR "firmware = none" ),
the completed
.BR wisp.conf (5),
the LUKS table when the root is encrypted, optionally a console keymap, a
module blacklist, a volume table, a detached header copy and one key
provider with its configuration. The CPU microcode is
prepended as an uncompressed early cpio (unless
.BR "microcode = none" ).
No shell, no network, no splash screen, no resume from hibernation, no file
system check.
.SH SUPPORTED LAYOUTS
LUKS2 (aes-xts, SHA-2 or SHA-3 hashes) holding either the root file system
directly or one linear logical volume; a root file system without LUKS on a
plain partition; ext4, xfs and single-device btrfs mounted without options
(the default subvolume). Not supported: LUKS1, software RAID, multi-device
btrfs, a btrfs root that needs
.B subvol=
(there are no root mount options), LUKS on a logical volume, LVM without LUKS,
striped, thin or RAID logical volumes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wisp (8),
.BR wisp.conf (5)
